# Installing nallox

nallox installs from any Arch or Gentoo live CD. There is no installer and no live image. You unpack the root filesystem, build a kernel with the shipped config, and register it with the firmware. On UEFI you don't need an initramfs or a bootloader.

> Not yet tested from start to finish. If a step fails, open an issue and include the last lines of output.

## Before you start

- **Boot the live CD in UEFI mode.** This guide does not cover BIOS. Check with `ls /sys/firmware/efi`. It should list some entries. If it says "No such file or directory", you booted in BIOS mode. In a virtual machine, set the firmware to UEFI (OVMF) before you start.
- **This erases the disk.** Run `lsblk` and check the device names.
- **You need a network connection** for the downloads.

Set these variables once. The values below are for a virtual disk. For NVMe use `/dev/nvme0n1`, `/dev/nvme0n1p1` and `/dev/nvme0n1p2`. For SATA use `/dev/sda`, `/dev/sda1` and `/dev/sda2`.

```
DISK=/dev/vda
ESP=/dev/vda1
ROOTDEV=/dev/vda2
URL=https://github.com/pluh2332/nallox/releases/download/linux/nallox-rootfs.tar.xz
```

## 1. Partition and format

You need two partitions: a 512 MB EFI system partition and an ext4 root.

```
cfdisk $DISK
```

In cfdisk, pick GPT if it asks. Choose New, enter `512M`, then set its type to EFI System. Select the free space, choose New, and press Enter to use the rest of the disk. Choose Write, type `yes`, then Quit.

```
mkfs.fat -F32 $ESP
mkfs.ext4 -L nallox-root $ROOTDEV
```

## 2. Mount and unpack

```
mount $ROOTDEV /mnt
mkdir -p /mnt/boot
mount $ESP /mnt/boot
curl -L "$URL" | tar -xJpf - -C /mnt --numeric-owner
ls /mnt
```

`ls /mnt` should list `bin boot dev etc init lib lost+found proc root run sbin sys tmp usr var`.

Keep `-p` and `--numeric-owner`. They preserve the setuid bit on `run` and `passwd` and keep everything owned by root.

If tar says "File format not recognized", the download is not the real tarball. Download it to a file and check the size. It should be about 75 MB:

```
curl -L -o /tmp/rootfs.tar.xz "$URL"
ls -l /tmp/rootfs.tar.xz
```

## 3. Enter the system, set passwords, create a user

The root account ships locked. You have to set a password before you can log in.

```
/mnt/usr/sbin/nallox-chroot /mnt
```

This mounts `proc`, `sys` and `dev` under `/mnt` and gives you a shell inside nallox. It unmounts them when you leave. Inside it, set the root password and create your user. Replace `me` with your username:

```
passwd root
U=me
echo "$U:x:1000:1000:$U:/home/$U:/bin/sh" >> /etc/passwd
echo "$U:!:19000:0:99999:7:::" >> /etc/shadow
echo "$U:x:1000:" >> /etc/group
mkdir -p /home/$U && chown 1000:1000 /home/$U
sed -i "s/^wheel:x:10:$/wheel:x:10:$U/" /etc/group
passwd $U
exit
```

nallox has no `useradd`, so the account is added by hand. Putting the user in the `wheel` group lets it use `run`, and `/etc/run.conf` already contains `permit :wheel`.

Check that both passwords are set. Each line should say `has-password`:

```
awk -F: '$1=="root"||$1=="me"{print $1, ($2=="*"||$2=="!") ? "LOCKED" : "has-password"}' /mnt/etc/shadow
```

## 4. Build the kernel

Build on the live CD, outside the chroot, and build on the target disk. The live CD's own space is in RAM and fills up.

The Arch live CD has no build tools. Raise its storage limit and install them:

```
mount -o remount,size=1G /run/archiso/cowspace
pacman -Sy --needed --noconfirm base-devel bc libelf
```

On other live CDs, install `make`, a C compiler, `bc`, `flex`, `bison`, `perl`, libelf and the OpenSSL headers with that system's package manager.

```
mkdir -p /mnt/var/tmp
cd /mnt/var/tmp
curl -LO https://cdn.kernel.org/pub/linux/kernel/v6.x/linux-6.18.48.tar.xz
tar -xf linux-6.18.48.tar.xz
cd linux-6.18.48
cp /mnt/usr/share/nallox/kernel-config .config
make olddefconfig
make -j$(nproc)
ls -l arch/x86/boot/bzImage
```

The shipped config has EFI stub boot, ext4, NVMe, AHCI, VirtIO block, USB storage and devtmpfs built in, so no initramfs is needed. 6.18.48 is the version the config was made with. Other versions usually work with `make olddefconfig`, but the download path changes with the major version (`v7.x` for 7.x kernels). Add `LLVM=1` to both `make` lines to build with clang and lld.

nallox has no udev, so modules are not loaded automatically. A driver you need at boot (a Wi-Fi chip, for example) must be built in. Run `make menuconfig` before the final `make` and set it to `y`.

Copy the kernel to the EFI partition:

```
mkdir -p /mnt/boot/EFI/nallox
cp arch/x86/boot/bzImage /mnt/boot/EFI/nallox/bzImage.efi
```

## 5. Register the kernel with the firmware

The kernel boots directly as an EFI program, and the root partition goes on its command line:

```
PARTUUID=$(blkid -s PARTUUID -o value $ROOTDEV)
efibootmgr --create --disk $DISK --part 1 --label nallox \
  --loader '\EFI\nallox\bzImage.efi' \
  --unicode "root=PARTUUID=$PARTUUID rw"
efibootmgr
```


## 6. Reboot

```
cd /
umount -R /mnt
reboot
```

Remove the live CD. nallox starts `ninit` as `/sbin/init`, which mounts the virtual filesystems and runs `/etc/rc`. Log in as the user you created.

## 7. After the first boot

Extra software comes from pkgsrc through the `n-pkg` wrapper:

```
run n-pkg setup
run n-pkg add gmake
```

`n-pkg setup` downloads pkgsrc, bootstraps it into `/usr/pkg` and sets up `PATH`. It needs a network connection and takes a while. Install `gmake` if you want to build kernels inside nallox. Other commands: `n-pkg add NAME`, `remove`, `search`, `list`, `log NAME` and `status`.


## Notes

- **BIOS machines** are not covered. For BIOS, install Limine or extlinux and point it at the same kernel with the same `root=` command line.
- **initramfs** is not needed. If you want one anyway, `dracut` or `mkinitcpio` from the live CD can build it, and you add `initrd=` to the boot entry.
- **Locked root:** the tarball ships `root` locked (`*` in `/etc/shadow`). Section 3 is what lets you log in.
